  "schema": "elysium.research.frida.recipe.v1",
  "name": "life_rig_resolution",
  "description": "Name the rig every player action resolves against, across weapons, models and sex, in one interactive session. Three answers ride together. The first is the rig set: CBaseAnimating::GetModelPtr resolves slot -1 (the body's own model) and slots 0 and 1 (the two extra animation-model slots VtMB adds), and each returned studiohdr names itself at Name@12, so a weapon swap or a sex swap shows up as the bank triple changing. The second is the selection: the activity ladder, the weapon's activity translation, the weighted and heaviest draws and the melee selector, each with the global sequence index it returned - an index into the concatenated space LookupSequence builds by walking the three slots and accumulating each one's sequence count. The third is ownership and blend: FUN_10089c40 recurses through the include-model groups until the index falls inside a bank's own NumLocalSeq@272, and it recurses again per autolayer, so every call names the bank that actually owns a contribution and the accumulate call beside it names the weight. Together they settle which copy of a repeated label retail answers with (docs/vtmb/animation_and_movers.md A.7), which the shipped corpus cannot decide on its own. The rig and ownership targets carry on-first keys, so each distinct tuple is recorded once for the whole session: they answer per entity per frame and interleave, so a key compared only against the previous call would suppress nothing. The player-state targets carry on-change keys, because there the timeline is the point. The discrete action and draw targets carry neither, because a weighted draw answering differently on an identical request is the finding.",
